<BaseLayout title="About Matthew Cochran — Product Strategy & Hands-on Engineering" description="Matthew Cochran connects product direction, requirements, and hands-on software engineering, balancing customer value with technical sustainability and business viability.">
  <PageHeader eyebrow="About / Operating perspective" title="Keep product intent connected to engineering." description="Matthew Cochran is a hands-on software architect who has led both product and engineering teams. His work connects the customer problem, the business decision, and the software that follows." aside="Product direction · Requirements · Hands-on engineering" />
  <section class="section shell about-manifesto">
    <div class="manifesto-lead reveal"><p class="kicker">The through-line</p><h2>Keep the reason for the work intact.</h2><p>Unclear direction, incorrect requirements, and lost intent at handoffs can turn engineering effort into rework. Matthew focuses on testing whether a build is justified and keeping that understanding clear through implementation and verification.</p><p>Product strategy gives engineering a purpose. Engineering brings constraints and possibilities that shape the product decision. Customer value, business viability, reliability, security, maintainability, and operating cost belong in the same conversation.</p></div>
    <div class="manifesto-copy reveal"><p>Matthew has led both product and engineering teams at artnet and OutboundLabs. His experience includes CTO leadership at artnet, Staff Engineer roles at Distributed Spectrum and Qrypt, Founder/CTO work at TruthShield, and hands-on architecture and engineering leadership at Medidata, Shutterstock, and other product organizations. At Medidata, he represented flagship products in architecture governance, architected brownfield modernization, and led teams responsible for product repair and delivery.</p><p>His recent work includes Rust/React radio-spectrum analysis, quantum-secure service architecture, MCP automation, and cloud efficiency. His current open-source projects, Junior Agent and Jevitate, are actively used across his own projects. Junior Agent reuses MCP/CLI tools from his earlier Praxec work.</p><p>He remains hands-on: writing software, designing architectures, creating product and project systems, and building open-source tools that make AI-assisted engineering more deterministic and accountable.</p></div>
  </section>
  <section class="values-band">
    <div class="shell values-grid">
      <article><span>01</span><h3>Product judgment</h3><p>Clarify the customer, consequence, and commercial premise before optimizing output.</p></article>
      <article><span>02</span><h3>Delivery control</h3><p>Model dependencies, critical paths, decision rights, and evidence so complex work remains steerable.</p></article>
      <article><span>03</span><h3>Engineering depth</h3><p>Use explicit boundaries, threat models, verification, and operational design to make trust concrete.</p></article>
      <article><span>04</span><h3>Leadership by participation</h3><p>Set direction, build teams, and enter the work wherever the system needs clarity.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="engagement shell reveal"><div><p class="kicker">Continue</p><h2>Explore the work behind the approach.</h2></div><div><p>At MC Product Labs, Matthew starts with focused customer groups and specific pains, tests assumptions before committing more work, and expands into additional use cases as the evidence supports it. His personal writing covers engineering and open-source projects; the company blog covers product problems and examples.</p><div class="engagement-links"><a class="button primary" href="https://mcproductlabs.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MC Product Labs ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="/#junior">Open source</a><a class="text-link" href="/resume/">View résumé</a></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
